<p>The six-colors folks do an end of the year evaluation of Apple every year. <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/02/2025reportcard/" target="_blank">Apple in 2025: The Six Colors report card – Six Colors</a> just landed. Read it for a complete roundup.</p>
<p>The condensed version: The hardware was on the whole great. The software not so much. Not too many fans of Liquid Glass.</p>
<p>My take? I have a new iMac. I love it.</p>
<p>Tahoe or macOS26 is the worst macOS version I have used since OS X became a thing. It is ugly, it is buggy, it is irritating and designed for the folks who love iOS. The increased congruence between macOS and iOS leaves me cold. They have gotten this wrong. The macOS was a “serious” operating system. It is now trying to protect the stupid. Every action I try to perform has an associated warning. Are you kidding me? I am drowning in notifications. “Do you want this application to have access to your files?” Yes. I installed it. Of course, I want it to have access to my files.</p>
<p>Liquid Glass. Who thought this a good idea? Why?</p>
<p>There has been talk about a successor to Tim Cook. Remember that the whole executive team contributed to the present state of the software. They didn’t push back enough to stop this descent into the swamp of Liquid Glass. I hear that the chief architects have left. But the whole executive team is responsible for this shit show. They are mostly still there.</p>
<p>Tahoe is preoccupied in trying to save the stupid user from him/herself. This is a fools errand. The stupid will always surprise you. You can’t design for stupidity. That manages to piss off everyone who is a little less stupid.</p>
<h2 id="apples-impact-on-the-world">Apple’s Impact on the World</h2>
<p>The comments on the hardware and the software in the annual evaluation were interesting but I was more curious about the last question: “Apple’s Impact on the World.”</p>
<p>The responses were devastating. People were unhappy. Got the lowest ratings in the last ten years. And the comments? Wow. Reading the comments gives you an insight into how the Apple intelligentsia thinks. It was informative.</p>
<h2 id="my-take">My Take</h2>
<p>I remember being a Mac user when Michael Dell told Steve to sell the company and give the money back to the shareholders. Everyone was using Windows 95, I think it was. Mac users were looked on with pity. The Microsoft train had all but flattened the Macintosh. I was a Mac user then. I was a Mac user because Apple aligned with my values. They cared about design. They cared about doing the right thing. They were getting their asses handed to them in corporate America but they were not going to compromise their values. I didn’t use the Macintosh because the Performa was a great machine or the Macintosh Quadra 610 was the ultimate in hardware. I used a Macintosh because Apple was a company who could run an advertisement saying essentially that if Picasso used a computer, it would be a Mac. If MLK used a computer, it would be a Mac. They could run that series of ads and no one found that unbelievable. Those were the values they embodied.</p>
<h2 id="today">Today?</h2>
<p>Apple is a giant corporation run by a CEO and executive team who value profits. That is it. Profits. There is no soul. There is no differentiation. They are no better than Peter Theil, Mark Zuckerberg, Larry Ellison, Elon Musk and the other assorted tech bro assholes who contaminate our environment and discourse.</p>
<p>Tim Cook and his minions stand for the new Apple. No morals. No higher ground. No principles. His giving head to this wanna-be-dictator and his group of fascists is all you need to know about Tim Cook and his Apple. They are one of the enablers of this monstrous regime. History will judge them for that.</p>
<p>My first instinct is to blame Jobs. He chose Tim Cook. He made an operations guy the CEO. Maybe Tim was the best choice he had.</p>
<p>The answer is not that easy. I remember Jobs had an informal agreement with Google to not poach each others engineers and keep engineering salaries low. That was collusion. Capitalists colluding against the workers to keep wages low. We deify Jobs, but in some crucial ways he was an asshole. I can safely state that MLK did not share those values, nor do I.</p>
<p>It occurs to me that I was the sucker. I imputed values on to the company which were not real. Apple was never different. It was a marketing ploy. Microsoft/IBM were the big bad ogres while Apple was the caring, design-focused revolutionaries who were fighting the ogres. That was great positioning strategy. That protected Apple from getting their ass kicked by faster, cheaper, alternatives. When the market situation changed and Apple became the behemoth, the masks fell off.</p>
<p>This realization leaves me profoundly sad. Rudderless and sad.</p>
